Hi Aaron:

I'm not a GIS geek, so the aspects of your question that relate to projections,
I will defer to my colleague Len.  You may also find his paper on survey design
(http://www.creem.st-and.ac.uk/len/papers/ThomasJCRM2007.pdf) of interest from a
practical standpoint.

It is important to understand the purpose of the design engine.  Fundamentally
it is intended to show the evenness of the coverage of a particular sampling
design.  It does this by laying your specified design upon your study area a
large number of times (because coverage is a property of a design, not of a
particular realization of a design).  The coverage probability is measured at
locations within your study area defined by the grid of points you describe as
your coverage grid.  It is this grid of points that is depicted in the study
area map showing the probability of coverage of each of the grid points in the
coverage grid.

After you have assessed the properties of your survey design, you ask to have a
single realization of your chosen design generated.  It is at that set of
coordinates that you gather data.  That single set of points is *not* shown on
the map produced by the design engine.  Those coordinates are provided as part
of the output text produced in the Results window.  You will plot those
yourself in a legitimate GIS system (like ArcGIS), because the GIS system
inside Distance is rudimentary.

Creation of the coverage grid requires that you specify the distance between the
grid points (cell spacing); note carefully the units in which your study area is
measured, and the units in which you are asked to specify the cell spacing.  The
reason for the message of 0 coverage grid points within the study area would be
most likely be a units problem.  Recognize that the distance between points in
your coverage grid is *unrelated* to the hypothesized detection radius of your
point transects.  The spacing (hence density) of your coverage grid, merely
indicates the fineness with which you wish to resolve how coverage probability
varies throughout your study area.

See if what I have said makes sense, then ask some more questions.

